1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,400
 In this video, we'll be taking a look at if else.

2
00:00:04,400 --> 00:00:10,240
 I'll be discussing a flow control using if else with operators and statement initialization.

3
00:00:10,240 --> 00:00:14,700
 So first, we need to talk about flow control.

4
00:00:14,700 --> 00:00:18,940
 Program code executes line by line, and flow control is a way to interrupt this line by

5
00:00:18,940 --> 00:00:20,180
 line process.

6
00:00:20,180 --> 00:00:24,280
 We can have different lines of code that can be executed based on different conditions.

7
00:00:24,280 --> 00:00:28,280
 And these conditions are programmer defined, and you can make as many conditions as you need

8
00:00:28,280 --> 00:00:34,960
 in order to get your program to run and the job completed.

9
00:00:34,960 --> 00:00:37,560
 So this is what an if and an if else looks like.

10
00:00:37,560 --> 00:00:41,760
 We have the if keyword, and then some kind of condition.

11
00:00:41,760 --> 00:00:44,960
 The condition must evaluate to either true or false.

12
00:00:44,960 --> 00:00:48,880
 If it's true, code will be executed inside this block.

13
00:00:48,880 --> 00:00:51,080
 And so you can do whatever you want in here.

14
00:00:51,080 --> 00:00:52,340
 Here we have an if else.

15
00:00:52,340 --> 00:00:56,360
 So same as above, we have if some condition.

16
00:00:56,360 --> 00:00:59,080
 We'll do some code here if that's true.

17
00:00:59,080 --> 00:01:03,000
 If it's false, else, then we'll do something else in here.

18
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:05,840
 So the first block is the true's condition.

19
00:01:05,840 --> 00:01:08,160
 And the else block is the false condition.

20
00:01:08,160 --> 00:01:14,200
 Let's take a look at an example.

21
00:01:14,200 --> 00:01:20,280
 Here we have if some username, which will be string, if it's not equal to a blank input.

22
00:01:20,280 --> 00:01:22,920
 So there is a name, then we'll process it.

23
00:01:22,920 --> 00:01:27,320
 And if the if the input is blank, then we'll display some kind of error message.

24
00:01:27,320 --> 00:01:30,920
 And on the bottom, we're just doing something similar without the else block.

25
00:01:30,920 --> 00:01:35,560
 So we only are interested in processing the username, and we don't do anything else on the bottom example.

26
00:01:35,560 --> 00:01:42,120
 It's also possible to nest else ifs.

27
00:01:42,120 --> 00:01:45,280
 So what we're doing here is we're checking someone's age.

28
00:01:45,280 --> 00:01:51,040
 So this entire thing here is going to evaluate to either true or false.

29
00:01:51,040 --> 00:01:54,560
 Remember that relational operators determine true or false.

30
00:01:54,560 --> 00:01:59,160
 So if somebody's age is greater than or equal to 21, if that's true,

31
00:01:59,160 --> 00:02:02,160
 then we'll permit their entry to a venue.

32
00:02:02,160 --> 00:02:06,400
 If this is false, we're going to go down to the next block here.

33
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:09,960
 So else, we use the keyword if again.

34
00:02:09,960 --> 00:02:15,440
 So else if we have parental approval, and if that happens to be true,

35
00:02:15,440 --> 00:02:16,840
 then we'll permit entry again.

36
00:02:16,840 --> 00:02:19,680
 So if that's true, that'll be executed.

37
00:02:19,680 --> 00:02:23,920
 If this one is false, and this one is false, then finally,

38
00:02:23,920 --> 00:02:29,280
 this last block will get executed, and we'll have a some kind of code where they cannot enter the venue.

39
00:02:29,280 --> 00:02:33,600
 You can have as many else if blocks as you want for

40
00:02:33,600 --> 00:02:35,280
 whatever conditions you're trying to test for.

41
00:02:35,280 --> 00:02:39,760
 So you can have one, you could have none, you can have five or six, you can have 20.

42
00:02:39,760 --> 00:02:40,440
 All of that's OK.

43
00:02:40,440 --> 00:02:47,960
 Now let's take a look at how we can use if with some logical operators.

44
00:02:47,960 --> 00:02:52,560
 Here we have if has ticket, and ticket valid.

45
00:02:52,560 --> 00:02:58,040
 So this has to be true, and this has to be true, then we'll execute the code here in this block.

46
00:02:58,040 --> 00:03:03,880
 In this example here, we have if the funds are greater than the cost.

47
00:03:03,880 --> 00:03:08,800
 So this whole thing will evaluate either two or false, and the account is active.

48
00:03:08,800 --> 00:03:13,360
 So if that's true, and this is true, then we'll enter into this block here.

49
00:03:15,320 --> 00:03:19,720
 In this third example, if we're either using a debit card or

50
00:03:19,720 --> 00:03:23,040
 using a credit card, then we'll enter this block.

51
00:03:23,040 --> 00:03:26,080
 Since we're using or either one of these can be false.

52
00:03:26,080 --> 00:03:30,640
 So if this is false, that's OK, and if this is true, we'll so enter.

53
00:03:30,640 --> 00:03:34,360
 If both of these are false, then we won't execute the code here.

54
00:03:34,360 --> 00:03:39,280
 So as long as one thing is true, with all of these expressions,

55
00:03:39,280 --> 00:03:41,120
 then we'll be able to do see if block here.

56
00:03:42,960 --> 00:03:45,800
 And finally here at the bottom, we have a negation.

57
00:03:45,800 --> 00:03:52,240
 So if the quiz is not complete, then we're going to display an incomplete submission morning.

58
00:03:52,240 --> 00:03:55,840
 This quiz complete here, that's going to be a Boolean.

59
00:03:55,840 --> 00:03:58,280
 So that's either going to be true or false.

60
00:03:58,280 --> 00:04:05,560
 We can also use if statements with functions.

61
00:04:05,560 --> 00:04:10,040
 So here we have our if keyword, and we're calling the temperature function of the freezer.

62
00:04:10,040 --> 00:04:12,040
 So we're checking the temperature on a freezer.

63
00:04:12,040 --> 00:04:15,400
 And if that temperature is greater than zero, we'll display an alert.

64
00:04:15,400 --> 00:04:18,600
 And we can use multiple functions here.

65
00:04:18,600 --> 00:04:23,080
 So if the temp in the freezer is greater than the preferred temp in the freezer,

66
00:04:23,080 --> 00:04:24,920
 then we can display an alert as well.

67
00:04:24,920 --> 00:04:29,760
 And just like with the previous slides, you can use or an end to combine multiple

68
00:04:29,760 --> 00:04:30,560
 different function calls.

69
00:04:30,560 --> 00:04:36,720
 Go has a feature called statement initialization.

70
00:04:36,720 --> 00:04:39,840
 And what this allows us to do is allows us to create a variable and

71
00:04:39,840 --> 00:04:43,040
 do a comparison at the same time.

72
00:04:43,040 --> 00:04:46,960
 So here in this first example, we're creating the variable named I using the create

73
00:04:46,960 --> 00:04:49,840
 and assign operator, and we're sending it to the value of five.

74
00:04:49,840 --> 00:04:53,720
 We then have a semicolon and we can do a logic check.

75
00:04:53,720 --> 00:04:56,520
 So here we're creating I assign it to five.

76
00:04:56,520 --> 00:04:59,120
 We then check to see if I is less than 10.

77
00:04:59,120 --> 00:05:03,280
 If this evaluates to true, then the block will be executed.

78
00:05:03,280 --> 00:05:09,040
 If this evaluates to false, then we'll go down into the else block and

79
00:05:09,040 --> 00:05:12,560
 do something else in the second example.

80
00:05:12,560 --> 00:05:16,280
 We're using the rank keyword and we're getting the user rank function.

81
00:05:16,280 --> 00:05:20,400
 And this rank function will determine if they're an admin or user or guest.

82
00:05:20,400 --> 00:05:25,400
 If rank happens to be admin, then we'll be able to do administrative things here.

83
00:05:25,400 --> 00:05:28,480
 If the rank is manager, then they can do manager things.

84
00:05:28,480 --> 00:05:31,320
 And finally, at the bottom, we'll display an error message because for

85
00:05:31,320 --> 00:05:34,360
 this particular functionality, they're not able to do anything unless they're an

86
00:05:34,360 --> 00:05:35,560
 admin or a manager.

87
00:05:37,240 --> 00:05:41,000
 Remember with statement initialization, we need to have the if keyword followed

88
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:46,400
 by our initialization statement, a semicolon, and then our comparison at the end.

89
00:05:46,400 --> 00:05:52,200
 The comparison should always refer to the variable that you created in the

90
00:05:52,200 --> 00:05:53,640
 initialization statement.

91
00:05:53,640 --> 00:05:57,440
 When you do so, you'll be able to access this variable within this block.

92
00:05:57,440 --> 00:06:04,720
 Something closely related to control flow is an early return.

93
00:06:06,120 --> 00:06:10,160
 An early return is simply using the if statement to validate some data.

94
00:06:10,160 --> 00:06:13,280
 When the data is validated, you're able to continue with the function.

95
00:06:13,280 --> 00:06:17,360
 If the data fails validation, you return early from the function.

96
00:06:17,360 --> 00:06:20,280
 You want to do this because it maximizes performance.

97
00:06:20,280 --> 00:06:23,520
 And once you've checked all the data, you're able to use it without having any issues.

98
00:06:23,520 --> 00:06:27,960
 This leads to cleaner code and reduces the amount of bugs that you have in your

99
00:06:27,960 --> 00:06:28,360
 program.

100
00:06:28,360 --> 00:06:33,680
 Let's take a look at an example.

101
00:06:33,680 --> 00:06:40,200
 Here we have a get session function and it returns a session token or an error.

102
00:06:40,200 --> 00:06:43,720
 The first thing we want to do is use if to check the error.

103
00:06:43,720 --> 00:06:48,640
 So if the error is not nil, remember that nil is nothing.

104
00:06:48,640 --> 00:06:52,400
 So if it's not nothing, meaning we do have some kind of error, then we'll

105
00:06:52,400 --> 00:06:54,760
 return immediately out of the function.

106
00:06:54,760 --> 00:06:59,400
 That's because this token will not be valid because some kind of error occurred.

107
00:06:59,400 --> 00:07:01,280
 The same thing down here with get cart.

108
00:07:01,280 --> 00:07:05,160
 So here we use our existing token in our get cart function.

109
00:07:05,160 --> 00:07:08,240
 And if that's successful, then we get a cart.

110
00:07:08,240 --> 00:07:11,320
 However, if we have an error, we need to check that.

111
00:07:11,320 --> 00:07:14,000
 And if we get an error back, then we should return from the function.

112
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:20,760
 If both of the error from get session and get cart, if both of those are nil,

113
00:07:20,760 --> 00:07:24,680
 it will end up down here at the bottom and will have valid token data and

114
00:07:24,680 --> 00:07:28,560
 valid cart data, this makes it okay to process a shopping cart.

115
00:07:30,600 --> 00:07:34,840
 This if air is not equal to nil, we'll see that a lot in your go code.

116
00:07:34,840 --> 00:07:37,640
 Because when functions return an error, you always need to check to see if

117
00:07:37,640 --> 00:07:39,040
 that error value exists.

118
00:07:39,040 --> 00:07:41,600
 And if it does, you want to return from the function.

119
00:07:41,600 --> 00:07:43,600
 And you can also perform logging in here if you like.

120
00:07:43,600 --> 00:07:48,880
 Let's recap.

121
00:07:48,880 --> 00:07:51,960
 Flow controls a way to change which lines of code execute.

122
00:07:51,960 --> 00:07:55,280
 If else can be used to conditionally execute code.

123
00:07:55,280 --> 00:07:58,880
 And we can also use else if for testing multiple different conditions.

124
00:08:00,480 --> 00:08:03,720
 If statements can be used with return values from function calls and

125
00:08:03,720 --> 00:08:06,640
 logical inequality operators can also be used with if statements.

126
00:08:06,640 --> 00:08:12,000
 Statement initialization allows for a variable to be created and tested within

127
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:14,280
 the scope of an if else block.

128
00:08:14,280 --> 00:08:17,240
 And early returns should always be used when possible for

129
00:08:17,240 --> 00:08:18,800
 efficiency and code clarity.

